# GIT + VS CODE TERMINAL CHEAT SHEET

# Windows PowerShell | HTML | CSS | GitHub

========================================

1. CHECK YOUR PROJECT
   ========================================

Show current folder:
pwd

List files and folders:
ls

Check Git status:
git status

Check connected GitHub repository:
git remote -v

========================================
2. NAVIGATE FOLDERS
===================

Go into a folder:
cd foldername

Go up one folder:
cd ..

Go to Desktop:
cd "C:\Users\didri\Desktop"

Clear terminal:
clear

Open current folder in VS Code:
code .

========================================
3. CREATE AND OPEN FILES
========================

Create an HTML file:
New-Item index.html -ItemType File

Create a CSS file:
New-Item style.css -ItemType File

Create a JavaScript file:
New-Item script.js -ItemType File

Create a folder:
mkdir images

Open HTML file in VS Code:
code index.html

Open CSS file in VS Code:
code style.css

Open JavaScript file in VS Code:
code script.js

Open HTML file in browser:
start index.html

========================================
4. GIT COMMANDS
===============

Initialize Git:
git init

Check status:
git status

Show changes not yet staged:
git diff

Show staged changes:
git diff --cached

Stage one HTML file:
git add index.html

Stage one CSS file:
git add style.css

Stage multiple specific files:
git add index.html style.css

Stage all changes in current folder:
git add .

Unstage a file without deleting edits:
git restore --staged index.html

Commit staged changes:
git commit -m "Update website"

Upload commits to GitHub:
git push

Download and integrate changes from GitHub:
git pull

Show recent commits:
git log --oneline

Show GitHub connection:
git remote -v

========================================
5. UPLOAD ONLY THE FILES YOU WANT
=================================

Check changed files:
git status

Stage only the files you want:
git add index.html style.css

Review selected changes:
git diff --cached

Commit selected changes:
git commit -m "Update HTML and CSS"

Push commits to GitHub:
git push

IMPORTANT:
Only the staged changes go into this commit.
However, git push sends ALL unpushed commits
on the current branch, not just one file.

========================================
6. DAILY WINDOWS AND MAC WORKFLOW
=================================

BEFORE STARTING WORK:

git status
git pull

CODE YOUR WEBSITE:

Edit HTML and CSS in VS Code.
Save with Ctrl + S on Windows.
Save with Cmd + S on Mac.

WHEN FINISHED:

git status
git add index.html style.css
git diff --cached
git commit -m "Update website"
git push

WHEN SWITCHING COMPUTERS:

1. Save your work.
2. Commit and push on the computer you're leaving.
3. Open the same repository on the other computer.
4. Run git pull.
5. Continue coding.

========================================
7. CLONE A REPOSITORY
=====================

Clone a GitHub repository:

git clone https://github.com/USERNAME/REPOSITORY.git

IMPORTANT:
Replace the URL with your actual repository URL.
Run this from the parent folder where you want
the new project folder to be created.
Clone only once per computer.

========================================
8. CONNECT AN EXISTING LOCAL PROJECT
====================================

Initialize Git:
git init

Add your GitHub repository:
git remote add origin https://github.com/USERNAME/REPOSITORY.git

Rename your branch:
git branch -M main

Stage files:
git add index.html style.css

Commit:
git commit -m "Initial commit"

Push:
git push -u origin main

IMPORTANT:
Use this setup only if you need to connect an
existing local project to a remote repository.
If you already cloned the repository, do not
repeat these setup commands.

========================================
9. COMMON PROBLEMS
==================

"Not a git repository":
Check your folder using pwd and ls.
Navigate into the correct project folder.

"Nothing to commit":
Run git status and check your changes.

"Push rejected":
Check the error message and whether GitHub
has commits you don't have locally.
Save your local work before pulling.

"code is not recognized":
Open files through the VS Code Explorer.

========================================
10. IMPORTANT SECURITY RULES
============================

Do not upload passwords or API keys.

Keep .env files out of GitHub.

Do not upload node_modules or .venv folders.

Use .gitignore for files you don't want tracked.

Always check git status before committing.

Always pull before starting work on the
other computer, after pushing from the first.

========================================
END OF CHEAT SHEET
==================
